/*
We count permutations p of {1..n} with
    sum_i |p[i] - i| = k

Observation:
For any permutation, sum_i |p[i]-i| is always even.
Let dp[i][j] = number of ways after processing first i positions/values
in a standard interval-DP formulation.

A well-known transformation:
If we build a permutation by pairing "opens" and "closes" while scanning
positions 1..n, then half of the total oddness equals the total number
of currently open intervals over all steps.

This yields the DP:
dp[0][0][0] = 1
For each position x = 1..n, with currently j open intervals and accumulated
half-cost s:
- start nothing, end nothing:          * (j+1)
- start one, end nothing:              * 1        => open +1
- start nothing, end one:              * j        => open -1
- start one, end one:
    * close one and open one separate: * j        => open unchanged
The total multiplicity for staying at j is (j+1)^2:
    - match x with itself
    - attach x to one of j opens or closes symmetrically
More compact recurrence:
new[j]   += old[j]   * (j+1)^2
new[j+1] += old[j]   * 1
new[j-1] += old[j]   * j^2   (for j>0)
and every transition adds cost j to the half-cost.

This is the standard DP for permutations by total displacement.
Finally answer is dp[n][0][k/2], and 0 if k odd.

Constraints are not stated, so we use O(n * n * (k/2)) time and O(n*(k/2)) memory.
Use big integers? Usually these problems expect exact integer within 64-bit or modulo,
but no modulo is given, so use arbitrary precision via boost::multiprecision::cpp_int.
*/

#include <boost/multiprecision/cpp_int.hpp>
using boost::multiprecision::cpp_int;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, k;
    if (!(cin >> n >> k)) return 0;

    if (k % 2) {
        cout << 0 << '\n';
        return 0;
    }

    int S = k / 2;

    vector<vector<cpp_int>> dp(n + 2, vector<cpp_int>(S + 1)), ndp(n + 2, vector<cpp_int>(S + 1));
    dp[0][0] = 1;

    for (int pos = 0; pos < n; ++pos) {
        for (int j = 0; j <= n; ++j)
            for (int s = 0; s <= S; ++s)
                ndp[j][s] = 0;

        for (int open = 0; open <= n; ++open) {
            for (int s = 0; s <= S; ++s) {
                if (dp[open][s] == 0) continue;
                if (s + open > S) continue;

                cpp_int cur = dp[open][s];
                int ns = s + open;

                // stay at same number of open intervals
                ndp[open][ns] += cur * cpp_int(1LL * (open + 1) * (open + 1));

                // open a new interval
                if (open + 1 <= n)
                    ndp[open + 1][ns] += cur;

                // close an existing interval
                if (open > 0)
                    ndp[open - 1][ns] += cur * cpp_int(1LL * open * open);
            }
        }
        dp.swap(ndp);
    }

    cout << dp[0][S] << '\n';
    return 0;
}

